plan Operations
title   Operations: contracts, reads, commits, write lanes and streams
status  proposed
goal
    App authors declare keyed reads, explicit commits and continuing streams. Readers share current data, edits survive
    racing reads, and every admitted wait has an honest ending.

    **Why now:** several actors read the same keyed data and write racing edits, so sharing, freshness and write endings
    are fixed once here.

    **Read first:** @spec.Reads and @spec.WriteLanes, the Specs built from this Plan.
outcomes
    O1  Contracts and keys
        Resources, transactions and streams are declared as inert contracts; constructing one starts no work.

        resource({ id, input, output?, key, services, lookup, staleTime, gcTime, persist? }), transaction({ id, input,
        output?, key, services, concurrency, commit, progress?, idempotency?, guarded?, previous? }) and stream({ id,
        input, output, key, services, delivers, subscribe, position?, catchUp?, reconnect?, idleTime? }) return inert
        contracts, written in *.contracts.ts; constructing one starts no work.

        Contract ids follow @plan.Machines.O1; distinct contracts with the same id reachable from one App fail
        construction. A definition or fragment declares each use once under operations (@plan.Machines.O1); Runtime
        activation is @plan.Runtime.O2.

        input and output are each a fields record (wrapped as Schema.Struct) or a full Schema; an omitted output is
        Schema.Void; a transaction's progress has its own Schema. The function is inline: lookup(input, ctx) and
        commit(input, { attempt, basedOn, progress }) return Effects; subscribe(input, { from }) returns a Stream and
        catchUp(input, { from, next }) an Effect.

        Contexts carry no signal: Flow interrupts the function's fiber, and an adapter that needs an AbortSignal takes
        Effect's own (Effect.tryPromise's or Effect.callback's, or Effect.abortSignal under Effect.scoped). services
        lists the services the function calls, is required on every contract and is checked against the function's R (a
        missing one is a type error); the Runtime's boot refuses by name an operation whose service its Layer
        lacks (@plan.Runtime.O2).

        Value, error and service types remain intact through declarations and use. Persisted resources require output;
        domain reads require it under @plan.EntityDomains.O1; boundary transfer is @plan.ServerRendering.O3.

        Typed errors never cross those boundaries. Resource persist defaults to false; transactions and streams have no
        persist option. Storage is @plan.Persistence.O2.

        `staleTime` and `gcTime` are required nonnegative Duration inputs; invalid values fail construction. Zero means
        immediately stale or collected at the next turn boundary after the last hold ends. Infinity disables time-based
        staleness or collection, respectively. concurrency is required and is reject, latest, cancel or serialize (@O3).

        A contract that moves money keys the server by something it recognises: idempotency: "attempt" or the input
        fields it is, so flow check can warn on a money-shaped write keyed by the Attempt; a server may prune keys, so a
        re-send on an old key reads first, and Flow keeps no key age. guarded: true on a transaction lets a proof of the
        record's version end a quiet doubt (@plan.EntityDomains.O1).

        key is required on every contract: a list of top-level input fields naming which server thing a call is about,
        [] for one; a composite key lists every field. Its Schema is input's pick; there are no derived or nested keys
        and no default. A key field is required and has no default (KeyFieldOptional otherwise).

        Equal key records identify the same resource entry, write lane or stream session within one Runtime, regardless
        of field order. Any value carrying the key's fields names the key; extra fields are ignored and a missing field
        is a type error. On a resource every input field that changes the output belongs in key (KeyOmitsInput
        warns otherwise).

        A key is stored as canonical text; a value JSON cannot carry gets a stable name, so a bigint key 1n restores as
        "$1n" and names the same lane. A key's fingerprint (field names, and each field's Schema type side and encoded
        side) is part of the operation's version (@O3, @plan.Persistence.O5).

        Picked key values accept null, booleans, finite numbers, strings, dense arrays and plain records. Undefined
        record properties are absent; undefined array items, cycles, accessors, non-plain objects, lone surrogates and
        unsupported values are rejected without running getters or coercion. Negative zero equals zero; Unicode
        spelling matters.

        Keys have no cap on depth, nodes or bytes (D24, D27). Invalid keys report InvalidKey (unsupported values; never
        size) with the offending path and change nothing before work starts; a key that cannot be encoded is @O3's
        Unencodable. Later mutation of input cannot change an admitted key.

        invalidate and clear match a contract plus a partial key: every named property must equal the entry's whole
        property value; {} matches the family. Keys are exposed in persistence, payloads and traces; they neither
        authorize work nor reconstruct input.
    O2  Resources and reads
        Equal contract and key share data and one in-flight lookup across actors in a Runtime. An entry is fresh only
        while it has confirmed data, is not invalidated and its age is less than staleTime. Separate Runtimes
        share nothing.

        O.x.lookup(select) returns fresh data without a request, joins an in-flight lookup, or starts one, and points
        the operation's one lane at that entry. A one-off read operation has one lane (in a fragment, one per
        incarnation); a newer lookup releases the previous one, whose settlement is evidence (Superseded), so only the
        latest lookup's ending is delivered. Its row is @O6's resource lane for the entry the latest lookup named, with
        that entry's key, held until the next lookup or cancel, undefined before the first lookup and after cancel.

        One-off read lanes are not persisted, except the Interrupted ending a freeze or an actor's end parks for each
        read it stops. O.x.refetch(k?) starts a request when none is running. While one runs, refetch joins it if it
        started after the entry last became stale (invalidate, write or clear); otherwise one trailing request starts
        when it settles and the caller joins that.

        An entry never has more than one running and one trailing request, so a poll faster than its server still lands
        data. O.x.subscribe(select, { when? }) places the operation and starts a request only when missing or stale with
        none running. Time passing alone starts nothing.

        Automatic requests use the oldest live placement's input; explicit requests use the caller's. A running request
        retains its starting input even if that placement leaves. The verb must agree with the mapping: success only
        with lookup, value only with subscribe; one operation uses one verb (VerbMismatch).

        Focus regained and connection restored refetch stale or missing subscribed entries with no request running,
        ordered by contract id then key. Offline status blocks and cancels nothing. Invalidation and clear follow @O5;
        domain repair follows @plan.EntityDomains.O1. Flow never automatically retries a failed lookup.

        A read ends in success, in failure, or stopped by Flow. Its success: and value: see confirmed data only; success
        carries current data even when ordering rejects its request's result, and equal data still settles the lookup.
        failure: holds only the read's own error tags (exhaustive, or one event for an untagged error). A read's defect
        or interruption ends as Flow's own value, tagged by `_tag` (Defect, Interrupted), mapped under its own key with
        a required `otherwise` in a record (the key and value names are proposed in the Reads Spec's R9); a defect is
        also an actor issue. **Reads share the write ending rule** [decided: user 2026-11-12] option B extends to reads.

        A read whose mapper throws, or returns something with no shape, ends as that Defect, with the store's
        UserClosure issue. A mapping value may be Flow.ignore: no event and no handler; never on a stream key or a
        listen entry.

        Mappers receive (value, { input, key }), key being the contract's key record of the call that ended. An ending
        is owned by the activation of the state its transition names and routed current or late at admission
        (@plan.Machines.O6). A read declared .gone(tag) with .byKey turns that failure tag into a tombstone for its
        key's row (@plan.EntityDomains.O1). Unmapped failure leaves its lane tag and an issue.

        value fires for confirmed data at placement and subsequently only when it changes by Equal.equals; optimistic
        values and predictions never fire it. Restored or hydrated placements do not remap an already mapped value
        (@plan.Persistence.O2, @plan.ServerRendering.O5). A live placement, request or optimistic layer holds an entry,
        and a one-off read operation holds its latest entry.

        After its last hold, `gcTime` on the Runtime Clock removes it; later use starts missing. Releasing one owner
        preserves shared work until the last owner leaves. Passive reads never hold or start work. Cleared, collected or
        replaced requests cannot later change data or replacement placements.
    O3  Transactions, commits and write lanes
        A commit starts only from an accepted action and always ends in success, failure or unknown.

        Only an accepted action admits O.x.commit(select, { optimistic? }); activation, restore, hydration and state
        re-entry never replay a commit. select reads the turn's after memory and the event. optimistic, the commit's
        only option, reads the starting turn's after memory, captured at start, and applies on start; writes is the
        operation's option, contract.outcomes(mapping, { writes }), receives { value, input }, reads no memory and
        applies only on success, before its ending's event, even after the issuing state exits.

        Invalid success writes apply nothing and report InvalidWrite; the commit still succeeds. A success write never
        overwrites data that landed after the commit started; it marks that entry stale instead, so subscribed
        readers refetch.

        Domain operations have no optimistic, and their writes may only invalidate. Neither restore nor hydration
        invokes a commit.

        Endings. A commit ends in one of three channels: success (it happened), failure (this send definitely did not do
        it) or unknown (it may have happened). The commit function classifies each ending with Outcome.success,
        Outcome.failure or Outcome.unknown; a plain error is a failure; a throw or die is unknown with reason Defect,
        carrying the Cause, and an actor issue.

        Flow interprets no status and no error type. The runtime classifies each send from the commit fiber's Exit and
        whole Cause, outside that fiber, Flow's own knowledge first, then most cautious first. The ending delivered is
        the Attempt's: a definite failure after an unknown send of the same Attempt is unknown, StillUnknown {
        failure }.

        Flow's own endings are two values, each a Data.TaggedEnum discriminated by `_tag`, so Match.tag works on them,
        and each also a Schema tagged union. Flow.NotSent is the ending of a commit Flow never sent, with tags
        Cancelled, Restored, Rejected, StorageRefused, Disposed, NoPersistence, OldVersion, Unencodable and
        ModuleDisabled (proposed name). Flow.Unknown is every unknown ending, with tags Outcome { error } (the commit's
        own Outcome.unknown), StillUnknown { failure }, Defect { cause }, Cancelled, Restored, Interrupted,
        StorageRefused and Disposed.

        An author's failure tag may spell any name, a Flow tag's included: an ending's origin is its key and type, never
        its name. Superseded, NothingToResend, InFlight and Released are evidence, never endings. Flow has no timeout
        reason: a call's deadline is its commit's Effect (Effect.timeoutOrElse). **Flow has no timeout reason**
        [decided: user 2026-11-12] wide consult W1, option B.

        Mapping. The operation maps each ending onto a declarer event (@plan.Machines.O1) under four keys: success:,
        failure: (an exhaustive record over the commit's own failure tags and nothing else, or one event for an untagged
        failure), notSent: (optional, one event whose mapper receives a Flow.NotSent) and unknown: (one event, or a
        record over any of Flow.Unknown's tags with a required `otherwise`; either mapper receives a Flow.Unknown).

        Mappers receive (value, { input, attempt, progress, key }); a notSent: mapper's attempt is absent for a declined
        commit, which has none. Every unknown: target is a Flow.uncertain event or Flow.ignore. A bare operation maps
        nothing: its endings are evidence and its unknown is not retained.

        An unknown that is not retained (bare, ignored, or a cancel's own turn end) is released, and the store hears
        Released (@O5). flow check requires notSent: mapped only where it can occur (@plan.CommandLine.O5).

        The Attempt. Every admitted commit has an exported Attempt { id, operation, key, input, retryCount,
        mayHaveApplied }. Flow mints id, random, when the write starts; every send of that write is the same Attempt
        with the same id and input. The commit author puts id on the wire where the API expects it; Flow sends nothing
        itself and derives no other key. `retryCount` counts invoked re-sends; `mayHaveApplied` says an earlier send
        ended unknown.

        A definite failure resolves the Attempt: trying again is a new write with a new id. Input uses the input Schema.
        Domain prediction versions arrive as `basedOn` under @plan.EntityDomains.O1; otherwise {}. Attempt durability
        is @plan.Persistence.O6.

        The write lane. A write lane is (identity with its version, contract id, key), owned by the Runtime, not the
        actor; it is created by work and removed when Idle. Its binding incarnation (the actor, or for a fragment's
        operation the live incarnation with the lane's subject path) receives its endings and its members address it.

        States:

        - Idle;
        - InFlight, one addressable Attempt waiting to be invoked or on the wire;
        - Held, a retained unknown;
        - each with queued commits W (none under reject, at most one under latest, an unbounded FIFO under serialize).

        A turn's members apply to the lanes in action order in its validation step. commit: Idle mints an Attempt;
        InFlight or Held, reject ends NotSent { Rejected } and the transition runs, latest queues one commit (a previous
        queued commit, or a never-sent head not yet invoked, ends Superseded, evidence), serialize appends to W (no
        cap, D24).

        Under concurrency cancel a newer commit interrupts the previous one, whose ending is evidence. flow check warns
        when a transaction that declares `idempotency` uses `concurrency: "cancel"`: a payment written with cancel can
        land twice, and a user's `O.x.cancel` on the wire still keeps the Attempt Held as `unknown { Cancelled }`.

        retry(k?) re-sends a Held Attempt with its id and input unchanged (InFlight: evidence InFlight; Idle or
        resolved: evidence NothingToResend). When the addressable Attempt is resolved (success, failure) or released,
        W's head is promoted; an unknown, a retry and a restore never promote.

        Every Attempt minted, promoted or re-sent is invoked after its turn ends, whatever the turn's outcome, once its
        acknowledgement, record queues and (for a persisted re-send) its lane record's capture allow. Under reject,
        latest and serialize an unknown holds the lane until retry resolves it or cancel releases it; no quiet release,
        no automatic retry.

        A kept ending (refused twice, @plan.Runtime.O4) blocks its key until heard or let go: idle denies, a commit
        declines NotSent { Rejected }, cancel lets it go. Two write operations over one contract in one actor tree are a
        SharedContract (proposed name) error, so a lane per contract and a lane per operation name the same lane within
        a release.

        Members: commit, retry(k?), and cancel(k?) in actions; idle(k?) (InFlight: denies InFlight; Held: denies
        Uncertain; a partial key passes only when every lane it matches is Idle) and retryable(k?, { times }) (passes in
        Held while retryCount < times, else RetryLimit { retryCount }; denies InFlight or NothingToResend) in
        guards; get.

        On an unkeyed operation (key []) a key-less member names its one lane in every handler, a NotSent
        ending's included.

        On a keyed operation retry, retryable and cancel may omit the key only in a handler of an event that operation
        alone maps, naming that ending's own Attempt only; once it is resolved or released a key-less member acts as on
        an Idle lane; under serialize, only in the head's own ending's handler; elsewhere a key-less member is
        KeyRequired. idle and get always take a key on a keyed operation. Starting is static: a write starts only
        from actions.

        Delivery. Every commit, and every retry that sends, delivers exactly one ending to a live incarnation of its
        identity with the lane's subject path, or parked for it; delivery is counted per ending: a re-send whose
        lane-record capture is refused delivers a second ending of the same Attempt, unknown { StorageRefused }, and
        re-parking an ending is not a second delivery.

        A settled ending is never rewritten. Evidence by declaration: a replaced never-sent commit, a released lookup, a
        declined retry, a bare write's endings, an ending mapped to Flow.ignore, under concurrency cancel the
        interrupted Attempt's ending, a fragment's lookup whose incarnation ended, and a releasing lane's endings and
        queued commits.

        A throw from the outcome mapper at admission refuses the turn, and the ending is redelivered once after queued
        mail. A late handler may start a commit; it is an ordinary call owned by its transition's state.

        Compensation recipe: handle a late success at an ancestor by moving an idle state to a compensating state that
        starts the undo; try the undo's definite failures again as new writes after a wait, and reconcile its unknowns
        with O.x.retry of the same Attempt, each with a budget. Flow promises no remote rollback and never
        retries automatically.

        Persistence and identity.

        Restore and reload are @plan.Persistence.O4 and @plan.Persistence.O6: a first send's capture and acknowledgement
        are one batch with the turn that committed it, so a kill before it lands restores the state before that turn
        with no ending; an Attempt acknowledged once restores as unknown { Restored }, delivered once per new
        incarnation (a reload, or a return after an end); queued commits end NotSent { Restored }; a head whose own
        ending is parked restores as that ending, never also unknown { Restored }.

        A persisted identity's commit in a Runtime without persistence ends NotSent { NoPersistence }. Runtime disposal
        is @plan.Runtime.O6 (past its bound an Attempt still on the wire ends unknown { Disposed }).

        A reset of the actor's record keeps its writes in flight: lanes, holds and parked endings stay unbound until an
        adopting ensure.

        A lane is (identity with its version, contract id, key): a lane of an older identity version is re-keyed at the
        boot and blocks nothing (@plan.Persistence.O5); a lane of an older key fingerprint is an old-version hold; until
        it ends every commit of that operation for that identity declines NotSent { OldVersion } under every policy, and
        a keyed idle denies; the contract declares earlier keys, previous: [{ key }], giving O.x.previous[i].retry(k),
        .cancel(k) and .retryable(k, { times }), which name only previous[i]'s lanes; a current member never names an
        old-version lane; a previous key equal to the current one is refused (PreviousIsCurrent, proposed name); on a
        fragment operation another path's Rejected comes before OldVersion.

        Values with no form never fail a turn: a commit whose input or key cannot encode declines NotSent { Unencodable
        }, its transition runs and its mapper receives the value the turn asked to send.

        Domain writes: their store effects publish at settlement, before their ending is queued; no queue or many key
        array has a cap, so no commit ends for size (D24); a domain write joins its record queues when its Attempt heads
        its lane, never at commit (@plan.EntityDomains.O1); a store module's defect declines a write that would join
        NotSent { ModuleDisabled } until reload.
    O4  Streams
        A stream delivers every message or only the latest, as its contract declares, and Flow reconnects a failed
        connection when the contract asks.

        A stream contract declares delivers: "every" (never skipped or silenced, one per turn, or one page per turn from
        a read) or "latest" (newest wins, current only while this connection confirmed it); position names a field of
        output whose value says where each message is (every only); `catchUp` reads history from the bookmark (every
        only, requires position); reconnect: { while?, schedule? } declares that Flow re-runs a failed connection;
        `idleTime` (required exactly with reconnect, "Infinity" for none) fails a connection that says nothing
        within it.

        A stream's resume position is not an input field.

        Placement: O.x.subscribe(select) or O.x.subscribe.each(select) in activities. A session is one per (actor
        incarnation, placement path, operation, key); its select runs on every committed turn to find the key, and the
        session reads its input once, at start, so a non-key field never restarts it.

        A select that throws keeps what is placed, with one UserClosure issue each turn; one returning undefined (under
        .each, not a list) releases what was placed, with one SelectedNoKey issue (proposed name). A session belongs to
        its placement's activation; a released session's undelivered items are discarded with evidence, and a new
        session for the same (actor, operation, key) waits for a releasing predecessor.

        Two operations on one contract and key are two sessions, with a DuplicateSession warning. Flow runs connections
        only by the declared reconnect and `idleTime`, and sessions only by placement. A session that ends Flapping
        reopens once on the Runtime's "connection restored" signal (the one @O2 uses to refetch reads), with its flap
        count reset; it never loops, since a session that flaps again waits for the next signal.

        The default schedule is exponential, jittered from a well-mixed hash of the session id and step (no saved seed);
        it retries while offline and ends Flapping after a count of registered flaps. Its base, jitter, cap and flap
        count are one named Runtime default set from the bench (D25), run with Effect's `Schedule`. Sessions release
        within Runtime O6's disposal bound (ReleaseOverdue).

        The adapter says three things: a value arrived; the server is listening (Feed.live(), with { proof } that
        nothing was missed, { head } giving the newest position, or { restartAt } for a restarted log); the connection
        dropped (Feed.down(reason)); Feed.ping is a sign of life.

        Positions in signals decode against the position's Schema at intake; a failure is Defect. Feed.callback<A, E,
        R>(register) is shaped as Stream.callback with no queue cap (D24).

        Feed.multiplex({ connect }), library code beside it, holds one connection while any key has a session: add and
        remove on a key's first and last session, emit.to(key, x), emit.all(x), emit.fail(e), emit.fail(e, key) (the
        runner's addition) and hub.stream(key); a joiner of a registered key hears Feed.live() at once; the hub lives in
        the app's Layer per Runtime. A `catchUp` answers Feed.more({ items, next, through? }), Feed.done({ items, proof,
        through? }) or Feed.gone({ reason, items?, next?, through? }).

        Outcomes: every with a machine consumer maps value (required), pages (optional, needs catchUp), missed
        (required: Unproven, Lost or Reset, exactly one notice per interruption), fills (required: a memory field or
        false) and failure (required, exhaustive over E, with Feed covering Flow's Closed, Silent, Flapping, Defect and
        Interrupted); latest maps value optionally and failure when anything is mapped.

        Delivery is at least once: handlers store by id. Deliveries are bounded by what Flow has pulled.

        The bookmark is a Flow-private partition of the slice's saved record, per (operation, key), holding at and
        filled, and for a latest operation last and heard; it is not a memory field, has no patch and no guard reads it.
        Flow moves it when a value's turn is admitted, keeps it through Denied and Unhandled, and a Denied value moves
        it with DeliveryDenied.

        Entries per operation have no cap (D24); a re-created entry takes filled from the field. fills names a field of
        the operation's own memory (an array, a Record or a number): emptied while the session stays placed it is
        cleared (stays cleared), emptied by a turn that released the session or while unplaced it is wiped (catchUp
        re-reads; without it, missed).

        A handler that changes a placed session's key while the state stays put empties the fills field in the same
        turn; Flow never wipes it; FillsOtherKey (proposed name) flags a registration with no entry beside a filled
        field and another key's filled entry. O.x.refetch(k?) forgets the bookmark and reads now; O.x.bookmark(select)
        states a position.

        Waiting has no connection; `dropAt` is set on leaving Live or Repairing and when none is set, kept across
        connections that never register; a registration ends the not-live period, and `dropAt` is kept with a latest
        bookmark. Domain listeners follow @plan.EntityDomains.O1. Persisted bookmarks and reopening
        follow @plan.Persistence.O4.
    O5  Writes and optimistic values
        Writes to read data are validated together and published atomically, and an older lookup never overwrites a
        newer write.

        O.x.setData(select) returns { key, data }; data is a value or updater(current) => A | undefined. Updaters read
        confirmed data; undefined declines the write. O.x.invalidate(select) and O.x.clear(select) return partial keys.

        A source replaces data only if its request started at or after the stored data landed. `setData` starts and
        lands when applied, so older lookups cannot overwrite it. Accepted equal writes refresh freshness without value
        events. A commit's optimistic and a `setData` action on its own entries are type errors on a domain operation.

        A write batch validates all targets before mutation and publishes atomically. Any invalid target (InvalidKey)
        rejects the entire batch; the batch has no identity cap (D24); no match is a successful no-op. invalidate
        retains data and optimistic values, marks entries stale and refetches subscribed entries with no
        request running.

        A request started before invalidation cannot make the entry fresh. clear removes data and optimistic values,
        blocks old results and makes surviving placements Pending before reacquiring. Related commits still settle.
        Domain write restrictions and predictions are @plan.EntityDomains.O1.

        Every reader sees confirmed data with live optimistic updates applied in start order. Base changes and removal
        of one update preserve the others; predicted remains true while any update is live. Optimistic values never
        become confirmed data or cross persistence or hydration boundaries.

        The Attempt's ending decides:

        - success confirms the optimistic look;
        - failure, a Flow.NotSent ending and a superseded commit remove that commit's updates without a refetch and
          without overwriting newer data;
        - unknown keeps them, marked uncertain, and refetches the displayed entries once.

        The refetch is a read: if it fails the look stays marked uncertain and the next natural trigger reads again;
        Flow never loops, and `revertOnUnknown` does not exist.

        An unknown that is not retained is Released to the store, and a release before the forced repair removes
        nothing. Success retains the updates until `setData` or a lookup started after settlement confirms the entry.
        clear removes them with the entry.
    O6  Rows
        Each operation's row in a snapshot shows the state of its lanes, entry or session and keeps object identity
        until that changes.

        s.operations.<name> reflects that snapshot's publication and keeps object identity until its lane, entry or
        session changes; inside a fragment it is the fragment's own rows.

        A write row is { pending, uncertain, waiting, retryCount, attempt?, progress? }, plus outstanding under
        serialize, and { pending, retryCount, attempt?, progress? } under cancel: pending is InFlight, uncertain is
        Held, waiting is W not empty, outstanding counts unresolved commits other than the addressable Attempt,
        `retryCount` counts the addressable Attempt's invoked re-sends, `attempt` is the addressable Attempt's opaque
        handle (typed by its operation, unreadable, unforgeable; absent with no head; the user, 2026-11-12, Q1 B), and
        progress is set by the commit's progress(p) with no turn.

        An unkeyed write's row is plain; a keyed write's s.operations.<name> is a keyed list of the lanes that exist,
        each { key, ...row }, read by key with O.x.get(s, k), which is total: a key with no lane reads the idle row. A
        one-off read's row is undefined before its first lookup and after cancel. A live placement's row is undefined
        while not placed; under subscribe.each it is a keyed list read by key.

        Guards and callbacks read rows with O.x.get(operations, k) as views do. Resource tags are Pending (no confirmed
        data), Ready (confirmed data, no request), Refreshing (confirmed data with a request), Failure (typed error),
        Defect and Interrupted. Failure, Defect and Interrupted retain previous data; Refreshing always has data. All
        carry data and predicted, true for live optimistic values or predictions, and key.

        A stream's row, latest:

        - key, connection, drop, failure, value (empty unless current) and last { value, at };
        - every: key, connection, drop, failure and `catchingUp`;
        - the bookmark is in session records and Inspection, not on the row.

        Undefined emissions need an author wrapper if they must be distinguished from no value. Domain row additions
        are @plan.EntityDomains.O1.

        placeholder({ previous, input }) on a placement may supply Pending data when a new key has no data; placeholder:
        true marks it. previous is this actor's placement's last input and lane data, retained across exit and re-entry.

        Placeholders never enter the store or persistence. contract.get(snapshot, input) (proposed name) passively
        returns the lane-shaped view at that snapshot's store revision, or undefined. It creates, holds, refreshes and
        starts nothing; invalid keys throw.
    O7  Cancellation and cleanup
        Releasing or cancelling work interrupts it and runs its finalizers once, and a cancel acts according to the
        Attempt's history.

        Planned release interrupts the fiber and runs finalizers once without interrupt endings; adapter-reported
        interruption of a read ends Interrupted. Releasing a placement stops shared work only at its last owner.

        O.x.cancel(k?, { attempt? }) acts on the lane's addressable Attempt, the one retry names. Without `attempt` it
        acts on the key's current request. With `attempt`, a handle from the row view or an Attempt, it acts only when
        that handle names the addressable head of a lane the key names; otherwise it changes nothing and records
        CancelMissed (evidence), so a cancel aimed at a request that already ended never hits the newer one (the user,
        2026-11-12, Q1 B). A queued commit has no Attempt and so no handle. On the wire it interrupts the fiber; a
        success or failure the call answers first is delivered as it is, with the cancel recorded as CancelMissed, and
        only an interruption that wins ends unknown { Cancelled }, queued when the fiber exits and retained (Held), so
        nothing newer is sent while the request may land (the user, 2026-11-12, Q2 B); waiting to be invoked it acts by
        history (never sent: NotSent { Cancelled }, and the next queued commit is promoted; a re-send returns to Held
        and is released); in Held it releases (Released, evidence) and promotes; in Idle it does nothing, with evidence.

        While a never-sent Attempt's acknowledgement is being written, a cancel waits for storage: stored, it ends
        NotSent { Cancelled }; refused, NotSent { StorageRefused }. A latest or cancel-concurrency replacement waiting
        on that acknowledgement ends the replaced head Superseded with StorageFailure evidence when refused, and the
        newer commit is acknowledged and sent; a pending cancel is never lost to a later replacement. That wait is
        unbounded, a stated limit.

        cancel on a one-off read releases the operation's wait, not the shared request; resources keep no cancel of
        the entry.

        cancel never drops a queued commit silently, with one exception: a fragment's release on leave (a key-less
        O.x.cancel in whenAbandoned) marks every lane of that operation for the ending incarnation's subject path
        releasing, interrupts an Attempt on the wire, ends queued commits as evidence, and applies later endings to the
        lane and store without delivering them.

        One write operation per contract per actor holds across placements. Actor disposal invokes nothing new and drops
        nothing: lanes stay with the Runtime (@plan.Runtime.O6).

        Cleanup failure reports an issue without undoing a published transition. Disposal failure follows
        @plan.Runtime.O6; Cause handling follows @plan.Diagnostics.O3. After cleanup, late results cannot change data,
        lanes, endings or replacement work.
verification
    V1  Type proofs preserve adapter types and reject invalid options, outcome mappings (a
        failure: key that is not one of the commit's own tags, an unknown: record without `otherwise`), missing services
        and key-less members of keyed operations.
    V2  Key tests prove equality, matching, canonical text, no cap and rejection before any side
        effect.
    V3  Stories prove shared reads, one-off read delivery, Runtime isolation and passive snapshot
        reads.
    V4  Clock Stories prove freshness, collection, refetch triggers and late-result rejection.
    V5  Stories prove atomic rejection on an invalid target, invalidation repair and edits surviving older reads.
    V6  Stories prove each concurrency policy's lane cells, queue order and declines, and cache
        writes before ending handlers.
    V7  Stories prove optimistic stacking, independent removal and confirmation after success.
    V8  Stories prove unknown holds, retry preserving the Attempt, cancel by history, no restore replay and old-version 
         holds.
    V9  Stories prove the Streams tables (session, registration, bookmark, delivery, latest,
        Feed.callback's queue, listen coverage, write progress, lifecycle) and their oracles.
    V10  Stories prove rows, retained data, placeholder isolation and historical reads.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        Choose key encoding and validation mechanics. Choose internal request and settlement identities and capacity
        accounting. Choose Attempt id generation and structural input codec details. Choose store layout, batch
        traversal and publication mechanics.

        Choose cleanup instrumentation. Stream buffering is decided by the Streams delivery and Feed.callback tables
        (C89); host-signal wiring is decided: adapters take Effect's own signal (C116).
